  "targets": [
    {
      "key": "macos.host",
      "platform": "macOS",
      "kind": null,
      "bundleIdentifier": "works.relux.tunnel.mac",
      "embeddedBy": null,
      "embeds": "macos.provider",
      "entitlements": {
        "com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension": {
          "status": "required",
          "valueByChannel": {
            "development": [
              "packet-tunnel-provider"
            ],
            "developer-id": [
              "packet-tunnel-provider-systemextension"
            ]
          },
          "portalCapability": true,
          "portalCapabilityName": "Network Extensions",
          "rationale": "The host manages NETunnelProviderManager configurations, and Apple requires the containing app to carry the entitlement alongside the provider. The value follows rule NE1: unsuffixed on the Mac Development channel, suffixed on the Developer ID channel."
        },
        "com.apple.developer.system-extension.install": {
          "status": "required",
          "value": true,
          "portalCapability": false,
          "rationale": "Only the containing app calls OSSystemExtensionManager to activate and replace the embedded provider. Host-to-extension embedding on macOS is impossible without it. Observed on all three shipping Developer ID hosts inspected on this Mac."
        },
        "com.apple.security.application-groups": {
          "status": "prohibited",
          "portalCapability": false,
          "rationale": "Withheld under least privilege (rule G4): no grant this entitlement confers on macOS is used by the selected design. The six grants were read out of the system App Sandbox profile rather than assumed - three are home-relative and break across the root/user boundary, three are system-wide and belong to mechanisms this project does not use (Mac App Store group content, a group-prefixed XPC Mach service, group-prefixed POSIX IPC). The host-to-provider channel selected by TASK-260728-7ii1xz is providerConfiguration plus a sendProviderMessage seed into a provider-owned system-domain keychain item, and its XPC candidate - the only one that would have needed a group-prefixed Mach name - was rejected. A private group container is redundant with the container every sandboxed target already gets unconditionally (application.sb:107-108).",
          "evidence": "/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb on macOS 26.5 (25F71): the entitlement appears at lines 302-330 and 459-465 and nowhere else. Full per-grant survey in appGroupLeastPrivilegeRule.survey.",
          "reopensOnly": "This row reopens if, and only if, one of three things becomes true, each of which is a grant in the survey above rather than a general appeal to App Groups: (a) the macOS host-to-provider channel becomes a group-prefixed Mach service, i.e. the XPC candidate is revived; (b) it becomes a group-prefixed POSIX IPC object; or (c) the provider is moved out of root into a user context, which would make the group container genuinely shared. It does NOT reopen because a spec sentence mentions App Groups, because a shipping third-party product carries the key, or to spare a portal sitting. Reopening is an amendment under section 11, naming which of (a), (b) or (c) became true.",
          "decidedBy": "TASK-260715-ypo7yo r4, on TASK-260728-7ii1xz"
        },
        "keychain-access-groups": {
          "status": "prohibited",
          "portalCapability": false,
          "rationale": "Withheld under least privilege (rule K2). keychain-access-groups is a SHARING construct, and on macOS this contract has no second member to share with. The host's own Data Protection Keychain vault (TASK-260715-379cpk) does not need it: the com.apple.application-identifier entitlement that the signing toolchain injects into every provisioned macOS target is itself an access group, items stored in it are private to the app, and it becomes the default access group when no keychain-access-groups entitlement is present. The macOS provider cannot be the second member - it runs as root against the file-based system-domain keychain, which has no access groups at all (rule K1) - and neither probe target touches the Data Protection Keychain. The r4 rationale rested on a host-owned pre-seed staging item held across system-extension approval; that item is written and read by the host alone, so the default group serves it. Persistence across an interrupted approval flow is a property of the keychain, not of a shared access group.",
          "evidence": "Apple, Sharing access to keychain items among a collection of apps (https://developer.apple.com/documentation/security/sharing-access-to-keychain-items-among-a-collection-of-apps): 'Xcode automatically adds the application-identifier entitlement (or the com.apple.application-identifier entitlement in macOS) to every app during code signing, formed as the team identifier (team ID) plus the bundle identifier (bundle ID).' / 'The system recognizes this app ID as the name of your app's default keychain access group by including it in your access group array ... Any keychain items stored with this access group are private to App One.' / 'If you don't specify any keychain access groups, then the app ID is the default.' Apple TN3137, On Mac keychain APIs and implementations (https://developer.apple.com/documentation/technotes/tn3137-on-mac-keychains): 'macOS builds the list of data protection keychain access groups available to your program from its code signing entitlements. These entitlements must be authorized by a provisioning profile.' macos.host is provisioned on both channels (rule R14), so its injected com.apple.application-identifier is profile-authorized and the default group is available on both. Both documents fetched 2026-07-28 from developer.apple.com.",
          "defaultAccessGroup": "262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.mac",
          "reopensOnly": "This row reopens if, and only if, a SECOND macOS target in this contract must read or write the same Data Protection Keychain item as the host. Today that would mean the provider moving out of root into a user context, because rule K1 excludes a root/system-domain provider from membership on technical grounds rather than policy ones. It does NOT reopen because the host wants a stable item name, because the iOS rows carry the group, because a shipping third-party macOS product declares the key (rule K1's shippingProductsAreNotEvidence clause applies here too), or to keep one cross-platform code path. Reopening is an amendment under section 11 that names the second member.",
          "decidedBy": "TASK-260715-ypo7yo r5, on reviewer verdict 04 F1"
        },
        "com.apple.security.app-sandbox": {
          "status": "required-adjacent",
          "value": true,
          "portalCapability": false,
          "owner": "TASK-260715-uyju7n",
          "requirementSource": "project-architecture",
          "requirementBasis": ".spec/platform-distribution.md — 'Link and embed Sparkle only in the sandboxed, hardened-runtime ReluxProxyMac host'; the sandboxed host enables SUEnableInstallerLauncherService and the documented <host-bundle-id>-spks / -spki Mach lookup exceptions. ADR-018 assumes this shape.",
          "rationale": "Apple does NOT require the containing app of a Network Extension provider to be sandboxed for direct Developer ID distribution. Verified 2026-07-28 on this Mac: all three shipping Developer ID packet-tunnel hosts inspected carry com.apple.security.app-sandbox = false (Tailscale, PureVPN, Surfshark). Sandboxing this host is therefore an accepted project architecture decision driven by the Sparkle design, not an Apple rule.",
          "verifyAtFirstBuild": "OC-2"
        },
        "com.apple.security.network.client": {
          "status": "required-adjacent",
          "value": true,
          "portalCapability": false,
          "owner": "TASK-260715-uyju7n",
          "requirementSource": "sandbox-consequence",
          "requirementBasis": "Follows from the project-architecture host sandbox above; a sandboxed process has no outbound network access without it.",
          "rationale": "Sparkle appcast fetch over HTTPS (ADR-018)."
        },
        "com.apple.security.temporary-exception.mach-lookup.global-name": {
          "status": "required-adjacent",
          "value": [
            "$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)-spks",
            "$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)-spki"
          ],
          "resolvedValue": [
            "works.relux.tunnel.mac-spks",
            "works.relux.tunnel.mac-spki"
          ],
          "portalCapability": false,
          "owner": "TASK-260715-uyju7n",
          "requirementSource": "project-architecture",
          "requirementBasis": ".spec/platform-distribution.md — 'The sandboxed host enables SUEnableInstallerLauncherService and Sparkle's documented <host-bundle-id>-spks / <host-bundle-id>-spki Mach lookup exceptions. Because the host has outbound network-client access, Downloader, Installer Connection, and Installer Status XPC services stay disabled.' Same two-value set in .research/260721_macos-self-update.md and ADR-018. Sparkle's own sandboxing guidance requires exactly com.apple.security.temporary-exception.mach-lookup.global-name with $(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)-spks and -spki: https://sparkle-project.org/documentation/sandboxing/ . Apple documents the key as the App Sandbox temporary exception that permits lookup of named global Mach services: https://developer.apple.com/library/archive/documentation/Miscellaneous/Reference/EntitlementKeyReference/Chapters/AppSandboxTemporaryExceptionEntitlements.html .",
          "rationale": "A sandboxed host cannot reach Sparkle's bundled Installer.xpc (CFBundleIdentifier org.sparkle-project.InstallerLauncher) without a global-name Mach lookup exception. Sparkle derives its per-app endpoint names by suffixing the host bundle identifier, so the value is authored with the $(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER) build variable and resolves to the macos.host identifier at signing.",
          "attributionNote": "Attributed to project-architecture, NOT sandbox-consequence. Two independent project decisions must both hold before this key is needed: ADR-018's choice of Sparkle with SUEnableInstallerLauncherService, and the project-architecture decision to sandbox the host. It is therefore NOT derivable from 'the target is sandboxed' the way com.apple.security.network.client is — an unsandboxed Sparkle host needs no exception, and a sandboxed host without Sparkle needs no exception. Rule X1 governs it as a reviewed exception.",
          "empiricalEvidence": "Observed 2026-07-28 on this Mac with `codesign -d --entitlements :-`. Six shipping applications carry this key with exactly the <bundle-id>-spks / <bundle-id>-spki pattern: Mimestream (com.mimestream.Mimestream, sandboxed, exactly these two values and nothing else), WhatsApp (net.whatsapp.WhatsApp, sandboxed, exactly these two), Slipbox (ai.slipbox.macos.app, sandboxed, exactly these two), BoltAI and AdGuard Mini (sandboxed, these two plus unrelated product endpoints), and Tailscale (io.tailscale.ipn.macsys). Tailscale is the same Developer ID packet-tunnel host cited in the App Sandbox row: it carries the Sparkle exception while com.apple.security.app-sandbox is false, which is consistent — the exception is inert without the sandbox, and this project's host IS sandboxed. Sparkle 2's framework binary in Mimestream.app contains three endpoint suffixes, -spks, -spki and -spkp; only the first two are entitled here, matching the spec's decision to leave the Downloader, Installer Connection, and Installer Status services disabled. The two-value set is therefore deliberate, not a truncation.",
          "notAnEntitlement": "SUEnableInstallerLauncherService is an Info.plist key, not an entitlement, so it is out of this contract's scope. TASK-260715-uyju7n owns setting it on the generated macos.host target, and TASK-260717-xempiv owns the Sparkle integration that consumes it."
        }
      },
