### 1052 — Bumping a contract revision without a procedure that names its live consumers is how one of them ends up authorizing a rejected revision (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r7)
- ROOT CAUSE: r5 stamped `TASK-260728-q5kjta`'s scope with "revision 2026-07-28.r5". r6 superseded r5 and **re-pointed one of two live consumers**, so the human authorization node named by AC5 went on authorizing the revision verdict 05 had **rejected**. Its mutation set was r6-correct — only the label was wrong. Reviewer verdict 06 F1.
- ROOT CAUSE (the real one, one level up): the label was a symptom. `amendmentRule` required bumping `revision` and appending to `revisionLog` and **said nothing about which live board records must follow**. r6's own fix — a board gate — was aimed at the *mutation* consumer only, so the authorization edge was the one live consumer nothing checked. An amendment procedure silent on its consumers produces exactly this defect on every bump.
- DECISION: `amendmentRule` now requires re-pointing **both** pinned consumers by task ID, and **R34 fails if it stops naming them or the word `re-point`**. The instance is fixed by a board edit; the class is fixed by the procedure being machine-checked. New rule **A1** (`authorizationNodeContract`) pins the authorization edge the way **P1** pins the mutation edge.
- DECISION: nothing about the pin is written twice. The revision phrase is a **template** (`revision {revision}`) rendered from the contract's own `revision`, and R34 rejects a template or A1 field that hard-codes one; the banned superseded set is **derived from `revisionLog`**, so a bump extends the ban automatically; the excluded-identifier set is derived from `c1AuthorizationScope`.
- FINDING: `notes` must be **excluded** from a staleness scan. Sweeping all **402** live board elements for a matrix revision label found two hits — the reviewer's finding, and this task's own `notes`, which the reviewer did not report and which is **not a defect**: append-only progress history where "r5 (2026-07-28.r5) closes all three verdict-04 findings" is a *true* statement. Same reason S1 excludes `revisionLog` from its count scan. The obvious "scan every field" implementation manufactures a failure out of correct history; A1 declares `excludedFields` and R34 fails if a field is ever both checked and excluded. The sweep is also what licenses the stronger claim: **no third live consumer carries a revision label**.
- DECISION: the verdict's offered alternative — a content digest plus revision — **rejected with reasoning recorded in the contract** (`digestAlternativeRejected`). A digest changes on every JSON edit including ones that change nothing the operator approves, forcing a board write per revision without pinning anything the label does not, and a stale digest fails identically to a stale revision. **The gate closes the defect, not the pin format.**
- FINDING (**a gate never observed failing is an assumption**): r6 introduced the board gate and only ever ran it in the passing direction, so nothing proved it would fail closed. 13 board negative gates now drive it from a *simulated* record — no live board record is mutated to test a gate — and each failure must be attributed to the **drifted consumer's own rule**, or a mutation to one consumer gets credited by a failure in the other.
- REGRESSION (found by those gates, in the r6 gate itself): the right-boundary check `(?![\w.-])` rejected **every** following dot, so `Also create works.relux.tunnel.ios.` read as *no mention at all* — the r6 absence checks were defeatable by a **full stop**. Now `(?![\w-])(?!\.\w)`: a dot violates the boundary only when it *continues* the identifier. Strengthens rule P1 as well as A1.
- REGRESSION (same source): a 220-character symmetric negation window let a neighbouring clause's negation launder an authorization — "`works.relux.proxy` is **migrated**" passed because AC4's earlier "explicitly **NOT** authorized" sat inside the window. A window wide enough to reach back over a list of four App IDs is necessarily wide enough to do this. Negation is now attributed **per clause**; clause splitting on `[.;:]\s+` and `,\s+(and|but|nor)\s+` is dot-safe because a dot *inside* these identifiers is never followed by whitespace. `negationWindow` was **removed**, not left as a field the gate reads and ignores — a dead field a gate still consults is the rot R32's dead-exclusion check exists to catch.
- SCOPE: one board field on one element — `TASK-260728-q5kjta.scope`, revision label only, character delta **0**. `TASK-260715-3jloqy` needed no change and still passes P1. No element created, deleted, re-parented or re-linked. No portal mutation, no product source, spec file, or generated project touched.
- VERIFICATION: **1024 checks** exit 0 (r6: 975); **130 negative gates** exit 0 (r6: 117), 13 new, all board-level; **57 preservation assertions** exit 0 including `c1AuthorizationScope` **byte-identical**, which is the assertion that proves the reviewer's "without changing the already-correct mutation set" was honoured; board gate **43 checks** over 2 consumers exit 0. `check-legacy-preservation.sh`, `task-board validate`, `swift test` (335 tests / 29 suites) all exit 0. The reviewer's own `jq` gate flipped `false`/exit 1 → `true`/exit 0. All three harnesses verified to run **in place** from the board resource layout.
- STATUS: handed off to review. AC1–AC4 met and preserved; AC5 pending the independent architecture reviewer.

### 1015 — A prose summary inside a machine-readable contract is a second source of truth, and it goes stale silently (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r6)
- ROOT CAUSE: r5 moved eight target rows, two rules, two assertions and the C1 scope, and left `crossPlatformRules[2]` still asserting the **r4** contract — the shared Keychain access group granted to `macos.host`. The r5 validator passed **846 checks** because not one of its 31 rules looked at the summary. The generalisable defect is not the sentence: it is that a summary *asserting* a grant is a **second, unsynchronised statement** of data that lives elsewhere, and nothing in a per-row validator can see the contradiction. Reviewer verdict 05 F1.
- FINDING: it was never one string. Sweeping **every** string in the r5 JSON for stale grant/count claims turned up a second survivor the reviewer had not named — `appGroupStyleRule.statement`'s "one App Group record **per family**", true of r4's two records and false since r5 deleted the probe record. Two independent instances in one artifact is a class, not a typo.
- DECISION: summaries become **checked projections**. New rule **S1** (`crossPlatformSharingContract`) gives each rendered sentence a structured rule; **R32** derives the grant set from the target rows, cross-checks it against the record's own `consumedByTargets`, then requires the `grantClause` **verbatim**, requires the target names *inside* the clause to equal the derived set, and requires **every other target named anywhere in the sentence** to be a declared non-grantee mention carrying its own clause. Display names are derived from the rows, and R32 proves none is a substring of another so a mention cannot be mis-attributed. Free prose can no longer smuggle a grant back in next to a correct clause.
- DECISION: **R33** extends the same discipline across artifacts — every grant clause must also appear in the rationale `.md`, whitespace-normalised, so hard wrapping is allowed and rewording is not. Two negative gates mutate the *document* rather than the JSON, which is the only way to prove the rule constrains anything.
- FINDING (**the new rule earned its keep on first run**): R32's record-count scan failed immediately on `appGroupLeastPrivilegeRule.ceremonyCostIsZero` — "**the two App Group records** are allocated at that sitting" — a **third** instance neither the reviewer nor r5 had spotted. A gate whose first run finds something its author did not believe was there is worth more than one that confirms what he already believed.
- DECISION: the corrected Keychain sentence states the exclusion **universally** ("to NO macOS target on either channel, in either family") rather than naming the macOS host as excluded, which is a deliberate divergence from the reviewer's literal rework text. **Naming a target is what went stale**: r4 named the host as a grantee, r5 moved the row, the name stayed. A universal clause is derived from all eight rows and has nothing to forget. Recorded in the JSON as `whyTheMacOSHostIsNotNamed` rather than left as an unexplained difference.
- ROOT CAUSE (F2, and the more dangerous of the two): r5 corrected `TASK-260728-q5kjta` — the **human ceremony** — and left `TASK-260715-3jloqy`, the **unattended task that spends that authorization**, on the pre-r5 contract. Correcting an authorization node does not correct the node that consumes it. Its AC2 required the packet-tunnel entitlement "**only on provider identifiers**", which contradicted **r1**, not just r5: Apple requires `com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension` on the containing app as well as the embedded provider, and R7 has enforced it on all eight target rows since r2. Provider-only verification would have *passed* a portal state in which neither macOS host App ID carries the capability — surfacing at first build, after the sitting was over.
- DECISION: the requirement is pinned in the contract as rule **P1** (`portalMutationTaskContract`) — required mutations, forbidden mutations, the two negative assertions, and the three **exact pre-r5 phrases** that may never return — and `TASK-260715-ypo7yo_check-portal-consumer.py` compares the pin against the **live board record**. R32 checks pin-vs-C1-scope; the script checks board-vs-pin. The reviewer's hand check is now reproducible: run against the pre-fix record it failed **14 of 20** checks.
- VERIFICATION: **975 checks** exit 0 (r5: 846); **117 negative gates** exit 0 (r5: 92), 25 new — 18 against F1 including a stale grantee appended *after* an intact grant clause, which a substring check alone would miss; 5 against the P1 pin; 2 against the rationale document. **53/53 r5 preservation assertions** hold against a pre-edit copy: all eight target rows byte-for-byte, exactly two top-level keys added, none removed. `check-legacy-preservation.sh`, `task-board validate`, `swift test` (335 tests / 29 suites) all exit 0. The reviewer's own `jq` gate flipped `false`/exit 1 → `true`/exit 0.

### 0945 — A least-privilege rule applied to one row and not its neighbour; and withdrawing a keychain group silently MOVES the default access group (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r5)
- ROOT CAUSE: r4 withheld `keychain-access-groups` from `macos.provider` on rule **K1** (the root/system-domain keychain has no access groups) and kept it `required` on `macos.host` for "the host's own vault plus a pre-seed staging item". Reviewer verdict 04 F1: neither half needs a *shared* group. The real defect is narrower and more generalisable than "an unused entitlement" — **K1 is written about the provider and is silent on the host**, so the host row was never tested at all. A rule scoped to one row reads like coverage of the class it names. New rule **K2** covers the host case, and K1 now carries a `hostScope` clause admitting what it does not govern; R31 fails if that clause is removed.
- DECISION: `keychain-access-groups` is now **prohibited on all four macOS targets**. Rule **K2**: granted only where a **named second target** in the contract reads the same Data Protection Keychain item. Sharing is the entitlement's *only* function — it does not unlock the DPK, change durability, or survive anything the default group does not — so any argument for it that cannot name a second reader is an argument for something it does not do. On macOS there is no second reader: K1 excludes the root provider, and neither probe touches the DPK.
- FINDING: Withholding costs the host nothing, and Apple documents it rather than it being inferred. *Sharing access to keychain items among a collection of apps*: the `com.apple.application-identifier` entitlement is added to **every** app at signing; "the system recognizes this app ID as the name of your app's **default** keychain access group"; items in it are "**private to** App One"; "if you don't specify any keychain access groups, then **the app ID is the default**." TN3137 adds the macOS half: the access-group list is built from code-signing entitlements "**authorized by a provisioning profile**" — and `macos.host` is provisioned on both channels, so the injected identifier qualifies.
- FINDING (**the trap**): the entitlement array is ordered `keychain-access-groups` **first**, then the app identifier — the first element is the default when a caller names none. So withdrawing the entitlement does not only remove sharing, it **moves the default group**: `262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.shared` → `262RZ595FP.works.relux.tunnel.mac`. An unqualified macOS write would have landed somewhere different before and after. Nothing is written yet so there is no migration, but `TASK-260715-379cpk` must not pass `kSecAttrAccessGroup` on macOS (it now returns `errSecMissingEntitlement`) while iOS still names the shared group explicitly — a platform branch, not a shared constant. **The verdict did not name this; it fell out of reading the source the verdict cited.**
- FINDING: Apple's documentation pages are JavaScript-rendered and fetch as title-only. The documentation **JSON endpoints** (`developer.apple.com/tutorials/data/documentation/<path>.json`) return the full text and are what made the quotes above obtainable.
- DECISION: `com.apple.security.application-groups` is now **prohibited on the iOS probe pair** (verdict 04 F3). r4 granted it because the probe "exercises the same shared-container path production depends on" — but `TASK-260715-1jckn0` scopes the probe to the `NETunnelProviderManager` lifecycle and versioned app messaging, its AC3 puts the whole exchange on `sendProviderMessage`, and no shared container appears anywhere. Rule G4 gained **`probeRule`**: a probe inherits **identifiers and profile classes, never entitlements**; if a probe is meant to prove a capability can be *provisioned*, that is a verification deliverable and must be an acceptance criterion **first** — the entitlement follows the criterion, not the reverse.
- DECISION: `group.works.relux.tunnel.probe` had exactly two consumers and both lost the grant, so **the record is deleted**, not deferred — a portal record no target reads is surface with no reader. Rule **G3** restated to the stronger property it now has: *no probe carries an App Group at all*, disposability by construction rather than by careful naming. One App Group record remains.
- FINDING: r1–r4 left all four **probe** keychain rows `prohibited` with **no reopening condition** — settled by *omission*, indistinguishable from an oversight and reopenable by anyone reading the silence as "nobody considered it". Found by the new R31 gate on its first run, not by inspection. Rows fixed, rule kept.
- FINDING: a negative gate caught a crash in its own new rule — re-granting the host *while* adding it to the record's consumer list made R31 raise `KeyError` (reading `e["value"]` on a granted row with no literal) instead of failing cleanly. Made crash-safe; a rule that throws reports nothing.
- SCOPE: `TASK-260728-q5kjta` (Ceremony C1) scope + AC4 corrected on the board (verdict 04 F2): four macOS App IDs not a singular pair, Network Extensions only, **zero** App Group records/capabilities, **no** Keychain mutation (there is no portal record), Mac Development profile class, all four iOS App IDs excluded. A downstream-consequence paragraph in the matrix is not the contract the operator reads.
- VERIFICATION: **846 checks** exit 0 (r4: 801); **92 negative gates** exit 0 (r4: 66), 26 new — 14 attacking F1 including the over-correction (an iOS row withheld must also fail) and re-granting *with* a laundered consumer entry, 10 attacking F3, 2 deleting assertions A17/A18. **32/32 r4 preservation assertions hold**, run against a pre-edit copy of the r4 JSON rather than argued from prose. `check-legacy-preservation.sh`, `task-board validate`, `swift test` (335 tests / 29 suites) all exit 0. Both reviewer `jq` gates flipped `false`/exit 1 → `true`/exit 0.

### 0912 — "Retained for a channel a future task will select" is a grant with no function; the sandbox profile settles what an entitlement is actually for (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r4)
- ROOT CAUSE: r3 granted `com.apple.security.application-groups` on both macOS production targets with the purpose *"the sandbox-visible shared namespace for the host↔provider channel selected by `TASK-260728-7ii1xz` **will** use"*, plus "so C1 need not be repeated". Reviewer verdict 03 blocked it: a possible future transport plus an operational convenience are not a current requirement. Both halves confirmed against the r3 artifact. The generalisable failure is **holding a capability open against an undecided design** — it reads as prudent and is indistinguishable from over-provisioning until the design lands.
- DECISION: `application-groups` is now **prohibited on all four macOS targets** (production *and* probe) and `required` on all four iOS targets — new rule **G4**: an App Group is granted only where a *currently selected* mechanism uses one of the grants the entitlement actually confers. Reopening conditions are deliberately **grant-specific** (group-prefixed Mach service / group-prefixed POSIX IPC / provider moved into a user context), so "the spec mentions App Groups" or "a shipping product carries it" cannot reopen the row.
- FINDING: What the entitlement grants on macOS is readable, not arguable. `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb` (26.5/25F71) contains exactly two `sandbox-array-entitlement` blocks for the key — lines **302-330** and **459-465** — conferring six grants. Three are **home-relative** and therefore break across the root/user split (group container r/w; `network-bind`/`network-outbound` inside it; `~/Library/Application Scripts/<suite>`). Three are **system-wide and do work across it**: `/Library/Application Support/AppStore/GroupContent/<suite>`, `mach-lookup`/`mach-register` on `global-name-prefix "<suite>."` (**lines 326-328** — the XPC channel, and the only reason the macOS group ever looked load-bearing), and `ipc-posix*` on `ipc-posix-name-prefix "<suite>/"` (**line 329** — POSIX shm, which no candidate ever proposed). "App Groups do nothing on macOS" would be the tidy version and it is false; the verdict rests on *these grants are unused*, not on *the entitlement is inert*.
- FINDING: A **private** group container is redundant, not merely unused. `(appsandbox-container-common)` / `(appsandbox-container-macos)` at `application.sb:107-108` sit at top level, inside no `(when (entitlement …))` form — every sandboxed target already has its own container from `application_container_id`, unentitled. The only thing a group container adds is *sharing*. Same for grant 2: `application.sb:754-756` already allows bind/connect on the target's own container. This answers the one question `7ii1xz` M6 left open, and answers it harder than M6 expected.
- FINDING: OC-4 gained evidence while being downgraded to dormant. `application.sb:326-328` builds the authorized Mach prefix as `(global-name-prefix (string-append suite "."))` from the **literal entitlement value**, whatever its style — so on the sandbox side an iOS-style `group.` ID would work as a prefix, contradicting r3's flat "no source addresses it". Not proof: the `launchd` bootstrap and profile-authorization sides were not exercised and nothing was signed.
- DECISION: **Ceremony C1 shrank.** No App Group record is created and the App Groups capability is enabled on no App ID. The convenience argument did not need weighing — it evaporates: C1's `explicitlyNotAuthorized` already excludes all four iOS App IDs under ADR-024, so iOS provisioning *already* needs a later portal sitting, and those four iOS App IDs are the records' only consumers. New rule **R30** makes C1 scope **derived** from what its App IDs require, so growing or shrinking it by hand cannot pass.
- FINDING: A validator rule caught its own author. R28 rejects conditional wording in a *granted* row's rationale; the first draft of the iOS probe rationale said "the same shared-container path it **will use** in production" and failed the gate. Wording fixed, rule kept.
- SCOPE: `PlatformVPNIdentity` (`Sources/ReluxTunnelCore/VPNManagerRepository.swift:17`) declares `appGroupIdentifier` as a non-optional `String` for both platforms — after G4 there is no macOS value to supply. Recorded for `TASK-260715-1tzaed`, not fixed; `production(for:)` still throws so nothing is broken today. `.spec/architecture.md:111` joins `security-claims.md` and threat-model M-02 on the spec-amendment list.
- VERIFICATION: 30 rules / **801 checks** exit 0 (r3: 598/R1-R27); **66 negative gates** exit 0 (r3: 41), 25 new — 15 attacking F1 including the **over-correction** (an iOS row withheld must also fail) and "the same defect one row over" (the probe), 9 attacking the `7ii1xz` amendment packet, 1 deleting assertion A16. 15/15 r3 preservation assertions hold. `check-legacy-preservation.sh`, `task-board validate`, `swift test` (335 tests / 29 suites) all exit 0. Reviewer's own targeted `jq` gate flipped `false`/exit 1 → `true`/exit 0.

### 0851 — An NE lifecycle read from the SDK header alone was wrong in the direction that mattered (TASK-260728-7ii1xz, r2)
- ROOT CAUSE: r1 concluded "the app cannot reach a stopped provider" from the macOS 26.5 SDK header for `handleAppMessage:` — *"called by the framework when the container app sends a message to the provider"*. That sentence names **who sends**, not a liveness precondition, and the header for the sending side says nothing about launch at all. Apple's published API reference does: `sendProviderMessage(_:responseHandler:)` — *"If the extension is not running, **it should be launched to handle the message**."* One inference from a header that was silent, and revocation was declared permanently asynchronous, `379cpk` AC4 was declared unsatisfiable, and a 60-second startup budget was spent waiting for a seed. **Generalisable: for Network Extension lifecycle questions the shipped header and the published reference are not interchangeable — the header documents the signature, the reference documents the behaviour.** Check both before designing around an absence.
- DECISION: Revocation is now specified as a **five-state table**, not a yes/no. Synchronous when the configuration is installed and enabled (running or not — the send launches it); unavailable and deferred to a start-time sweep when the configuration is disabled (`NEVPNErrorConfigurationDisabled`), invalid/unapproved (`NEVPNErrorConfigurationInvalid`), or when no host is running at all. Seeding moved out of `startTunnel` entirely, so the credential is normally at rest before any start; `startTunnel` is now read-only and fails fast with `credentialNotProvisioned`. The start-time sweep survives as crash-recovery defence, not as the deletion path.
- FINDING: The reverse dependency stays forbidden and is what still kills the memory-only candidate B. Apple, same page: *"you should not rely on this communication channel with the containing app for basic Tunnel Provider operation (start, stop, etc.)."* App→provider may launch; provider→app may never be assumed. The transport selection (candidate D) therefore did not change — only the lifecycle around it.
- FINDING: `kSecUseKeychain` is **add-only**. `SecItem.h` defines it as "a value of type `SecKeychainRef` to which **`SecItemAdd`** will add the provided item(s)"; scoping a *search* takes `kSecMatchSearchList`. r1 specified `kSecUseKeychain` for the query. Verified end to end against a throwaway keychain — add/read/delete all `OSStatus=0`, and a **negative control** with the search list pointed at the user default returns `-25300 errSecItemNotFound`, proving the list actually scopes rather than being silently ignored. This also gives `379cpk`/`1o9wjz` a real unit-test seam needing neither root nor the System keychain.
- FINDING: Do not hard-code `/Library/Keychains/System.keychain`. `SecKeychainCopyDomainDefault(kSecPreferencesDomainSystem, &kc)` returns `OSStatus=0` and resolves to exactly that path from an ordinary user context (uid 502), and `SecKeychainCopyDomainSearchList` for the system domain has **count=1** — physically confirming TN3137's *"in the system context the search list includes just the System keychain"*. The fix is behaviour-preserving, so there was never a reason to pin the literal. The whole `SecKeychain`/`SecAccess` surface is `API_DEPRECATED(… macos(10.2, 10.10))` yet still ships in the 26.5 SDK and remains Apple's only documented daemon option.
- ANOMALY: `Security.framework/Headers/SecKeychain.h` is **ISO-8859 encoded**. A plain UTF-8 `grep` over it silently prints nothing and exits 1 — indistinguishable from "the symbol isn't there". This nearly produced the opposite error, concluding the domain APIs had been removed from the SDK. `iconv -f ISO-8859-1 -t UTF-8` first when grepping Apple headers; an empty grep result on a header is not evidence of absence.
- DECISION: A settled decision row must have its **pending scaffolding deleted with it**. The ypo7yo matrix row still carried `resolutionOwner: TASK-260728-7ii1xz` and an `amendmentRule` reading "if the transport gives the provider direct keychain access, amend to `required` with the group literal". The selected transport makes that antecedent **true** while the file-based keychain makes the consequent **false** — so leaving it in place was a live instruction to re-grant the row on precisely the reasoning the decision refutes. The amendment packet now covers all five keys on the row field by field. Worth a validator gate: a `prohibited` row that still names a `resolutionOwner` is a contradiction.

### 0822 — macOS credential transport decided: the root provider owns a System keychain item, seeded once over the NE app-message channel (TASK-260728-7ii1xz)
- DECISION: Candidate D selected. Host reads the secret in user context, hands it to the running provider via `sendProviderMessage` → `handleAppMessage`, and the **root provider** persists it in the file-based **System keychain** (`/Library/Keychains/System.keychain`) with a restrictive `SecAccess`. This is exactly Apple DTS's recommendation for this shape (forums/thread/759976, /133933). Rejected: shared Data Protection Keychain group (cannot work), memory-only IPC seed (dies on system start), custom XPC Mach service (needs exception entitlements + makes OC-4 live), root-owned encrypted file (Tailscale's pattern — same security, more code, more entitlements).
- FINDING: The decisive fact is physically readable on this Mac. `/System/Library/Sandbox/Profiles/application.sb:792-793` (macOS 26.5/25F71, sha256 `2e711078…`): `(when (entitlement "com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension") (allow file-read* file-write* (subpath "/Library/Keychains")))`. An NE-entitled process already has System keychain access from the base App Sandbox. Lines 655-656 grant the `securityd`/`SecurityServer` Mach lookups to every sandboxed process. **The selected transport costs zero entitlements.**
- DECISION: `keychain-access-groups` on `works.relux.tunnel.mac.tunnel` — **keep prohibited**, promoted from `prohibited-pending-decision` to settled. Not "unnecessary" but *inapplicable*: the file-based keychain is ACL-controlled (`SecAccess`), access groups are a Data Protection Keychain concept only (TN3137). Applying owner is `TASK-260715-ypo7yo`; closes OC-1, downgrades OC-4.
- ANOMALY: Second instance of the pattern logged at 0755 — **shipping entitlements are not evidence of requirement**. Surfshark's provider ships a `/Library/Keychains/` temporary exception the current sandbox profile makes redundant; Surfshark and Tailscale both ship `/private/var/db/mds/` exceptions dating to a Catalina-era bug fixed in Big Sur (forums/thread/660123), which `application.sb:544-546` already covers for uid 0; and PureVPN's provider carries `keychain-access-groups` that Facts 1-2 say cannot bridge the user boundary. Three shipping products, three stale or inert entitlements.
- FINDING: **The App Group channel is broken on macOS too**, not just the keychain. `containerURL(forSecurityApplicationGroupIdentifier:)` resolves relative to the caller's home and the root provider's home is `/private/var/root` (verified root-private, `ls` exit 1). `.spec/architecture.md:111` says shared state is "an App Group configuration snapshot and Keychain references" — both halves fail on macOS. The non-secret snapshot must travel in `providerConfiguration`, which the accepted M1 lifecycle contract already does. Directly affects `TASK-260715-3f4lxy`.
- FINDING: One property macOS **cannot** offer that iOS does: the System keychain unlocks at boot from root-only `/private/var/db/SystemKey`, so the secret is **not** protected by the user's login password. ~~(2) deletion cannot be synchronous — the user-context app cannot delete a root-owned item while the provider is stopped, so revocation is a provider request reconciled by a start-time sweep.~~ **CORRECTED in r2 — see the 0851 entry.** Revocation *is* synchronous whenever the NE configuration is installed and enabled; the r1 claim rested on a misread SDK header.
- FINDING: System keychain **attributes are world-readable** — `security dump-keychain /Library/Keychains/System.keychain` as uid 502 exits 0 and prints 192 items' attributes. Item *data* is ACL-gated (`-d` blocked on an interactive authorization prompt, killed at 120s). Consequence: the credential item's service/account values must carry no hostname, account, or profile name.
- NOTE: Experiment design worth reusing. `ssh localhost` gives a **`Background`** launchd session at the same uid while the terminal is **`Aqua`** — a free, no-root way to isolate the "user login context" axis. It showed the file-based login keychain returning `OSStatus=0` in Aqua and `-25308 errSecInteractionNotAllowed` in Background. It could **not** test the Data Protection Keychain: an ad-hoc-signed CLI returns `-34018 errSecMissingEntitlement` in both contexts, because DP access groups must be authorized by a provisioning profile (TN3137) and Ceremony C1 has not run.
- BLOCKED: Nothing. Named limitation instead — `sudo -n true` exit 1 in a non-interactive session, so **no code was run as root**. Checks V1–V3 (V1 System keychain write from the signed sandboxed provider, V2 root group-container path, V3 system start with the app not running) were appended to `TASK-260715-9yp8to` rather than creating a duplicate physical-Mac node (ADR-028a). r2 added **V4/V5** to the same node.

### 0755 — An exhaustive entitlement allowlist inherits every entitlement the architecture implies, including ones the matrix only mentioned in prose (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r3)
- ROOT CAUSE: r2 argued in D-4 that `ReluxProxyMac` is sandboxed *because of* Sparkle, and quoted the spec clause naming the `-spks`/`-spki` Mach lookup exceptions — then never authored `com.apple.security.temporary-exception.mach-lookup.global-name` as an entitlement row. The r2 containment assertion A10b declares every present key must be authored or signing-generated, so the allowlist would have **rejected a correctly built sandboxed Sparkle host**. Same defect class as r1's F3, one revision later: prose acknowledged the requirement, the machine-checkable set did not. The generalisable lesson is that the moment an assertion becomes *exhaustive*, every architectural consequence stated anywhere in the contract must also appear as data — rationale text is no longer a place a requirement can live.
- FINDING: Verified three ways before accepting. `.spec/platform-distribution.md:103-105` mandates the two exceptions; this repo's own `.research/260721_macos-self-update.md:111-113` records the identical two-value set independently; and `codesign -d --entitlements :-` across `/Applications` found six shipping apps carrying exactly the `<bundle-id>-spks` / `-spki` pattern, three of them (Mimestream, WhatsApp, Slipbox) sandboxed with those two values and nothing else.
- ANOMALY: **Tailscale carries the Sparkle exception while `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` is `false`** — the same product cited as D-4 evidence that hosts are routinely unsandboxed. The exception is inert without the sandbox, so "shipping products carry this key" is not by itself evidence that a key is required. Worth remembering when mining installed apps for entitlement precedent: presence proves the shape is *accepted*, never that it is *needed*.
- FINDING: Sparkle 2's framework binary names **three** endpoint suffixes — `-spks`, `-spki`, `-spkp` — not two. The spec entitles two because the Downloader, Installer Connection, and Installer Status XPC services stay disabled. Recording this makes the two-value set legible as a deliberate least-privilege choice rather than an omission the next reviewer re-derives.
- DECISION: The row is attributed `project-architecture`, **not** `sandbox-consequence`, even though `com.apple.security.network.client` on the same target is a sandbox consequence and is equally Sparkle-driven. `network.client` follows mechanically from sandboxing alone; this key needs two independent project decisions to both hold (ADR-018's Sparkle choice **and** the host sandbox), so it is not derivable from "this target is sandboxed". Also added **rule X1**: the spec bans "unreviewed exception entitlement", so the contract now carries a reviewed-exception register plus an explicit banned set for Hardened Runtime relaxations — `get-task-allow` deliberately excluded from that set, since it is toolchain-injected on the development channel and governed by A10d instead. Without X1 this defect recurs one key at a time.
- VERIFICATION: 27 rules / 598 checks, exit 0, and **41 negative gates** (was 23) each re-introducing a defect and failing closed naming its rule — nine for the missing/mis-scoped exception, five for X1, four that delete or empty an assertion. That last group exists because the failure being fixed was an assertion set that did not cover its own contract; R27 now pins the assertion ids so the set cannot silently shrink again.

### 0730 — Entitlement values are a property of the signing channel, not of the target (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r2)
- ROOT CAUSE: The r1 matrix assigned `packet-tunnel-provider-systemextension` to all four macOS targets unconditionally while authorizing **Mac Development** profiles for the same four App IDs. Apple DTS is explicit that the value follows the distribution channel: the `-systemextension` suffix is for direct Developer ID signing, and "otherwise — including when you distribute the app on the App Store and when signing for development — use the values without that suffix" (forums/thread/800887). Ceremony C1 would have provisioned development profiles against a value development signing never uses, and C1 is a one-shot human node (ADR-028).
- FINDING: Verified against three shipping Developer-ID packet-tunnel products installed on this Mac — Tailscale, PureVPN, Surfshark. All three carry the suffixed value on **both** the containing app and the embedded sysex, confirming the Developer ID half. No development-signed counterpart exists locally, so the development half rests on the DTS statement and is re-checked by assertion A3 at first signed build.
- DECISION: Every NE value in the contract is now keyed by channel (`development` / `developer-id` / `app-store`), each profile row records `authorizesNetworkExtensionValues`, and the C1 scope is pinned to development-channel values so the ceremony cannot be pointed at the wrong one. Consequence worth remembering: the probe family is development-signed only, so it can prove the **development** entitlement shape and nothing else — r1 implied it proved the production shape.

### 0726 — Apple does not require the containing app of an NE provider to be sandboxed (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r2)
- FINDING: Read `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` off three shipping Developer-ID host/provider pairs on this Mac: hosts `false` in all three (Tailscale, PureVPN, Surfshark), providers `true` in all three. Apple requires the **provider system extension** to be sandboxed; the containing app is not covered by that rule for direct distribution. r1 attributed both to a single Apple requirement.
- DECISION: `ReluxProxyMac`'s sandbox is recorded as a **project** decision — `.spec/platform-distribution.md` embeds Sparkle only in the sandboxed hardened-runtime host with `SUEnableInstallerLauncherService` and the `-spks`/`-spki` Mach exceptions, per ADR-018 — not an Apple rule. The contract now carries `requirementSource` per row (`apple-requirement` / `project-architecture` / `sandbox-consequence` / `platform-inherent`) and validator R21 rejects a host row claiming an Apple requirement.
- NOTE: This sharpens OC-2. None of the three shipping products is a sandboxed host, so the sandboxed-host + `system-extension.install` combination has **no local precedent** to lean on; `9yp8to` is genuinely first through that door.

### 0722 — iOS-style App Group IDs are the correct macOS choice since Feb 2025, with one unverified corner (TASK-260715-ypo7yo, r2)
- ROOT CAUSE: r1 registered `group.works.relux.tunnel` but had macOS claim `262RZ595FP.group.works.relux.tunnel`, calling it "the same record with a team prefix". Those are two different Apple identifier *styles*, not two spellings of one record: iOS-style begins `group.` and is allocated on the Developer website; macOS-style begins with the Team ID and **cannot** be allocated there at all. The macOS literal therefore did not identify the portal record the contract said it consumed.
- FINDING: Apple completed Developer-website support for iOS-style app group IDs on macOS on **21 Feb 2025**, and Xcode 16.3+ makes it the macOS default (forums/thread/721701). This project is new on Xcode 26.5, so both platforms now claim the identical registered literal.
- ANOMALY: Every Apple example of an app group used as a sandboxed **Mach service name prefix**, and all three shipping products inspected here, use the macOS-style Team-ID form — and all predate Feb 2025. No source found confirms an iOS-style ID works as a Mach service prefix. Not blocking: the spec's host↔provider channel is an App Group snapshot plus Keychain references (`.spec/architecture.md:111`), not XPC. Recorded as **OC-4** owned by `TASK-260728-7ii1xz`, with the amendment path being a separate *unregistered* macOS-style ID for that one purpose. This supersedes the 0657 claim below that the macOS group is required "as the XPC Mach-service namespace" — that purpose is now conditional, not established.
- FINDING: The r1 D-2 rationale ("the entitlement would be non-functional") was too strong. PureVPN's root sysexes carry `keychain-access-groups` outright and Surfshark's carries a `/Library/Keychains/` path exception — i.e. the **file-based System keychain** works from root. What fails is specifically the *Data Protection* Keychain sharing the spec asks for. The row stays withheld because its purpose is undetermined until `7ii1xz` picks a transport, not because it is inert.

